<Project>

  <!--
    The SDK versions independently of the hub, and that is a deliberate exception rather than an oversight.

    The private product repo's own Directory.Build.props sets one version for the whole product, with the
    reasoning written above it: the hub and every driver ship together because that is the number a user
    reads out to you when something is wrong, and "a matrix of independently versioned parts is unanswerable
    over the phone."

    That rule is right for a thing you install as a unit and wrong for a thing strangers compile against.
    A plugin built against SDK 1.2 has to keep working on hub 1.7 — the whole point of a published contract
    is that the two move apart — so a package stamped with the product's date-based CI version
    (YYYY.MM.0-<channel>.<run>.g<sha>) would be unusable to anyone outside the build that produced it.

    So: plain semver here, and the *protocol* carries the compatibility statement rather than the package
    version. Which proto a plugin was built against is the question that matters, and it is answered on the
    wire, not by a NuGet range.

    Keep it plain. No `+` build metadata — it breaks Docker tags and mangles artifact URLs, which the
    product repo learned once already.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>0.1.0</VersionPrefix>
    <VersionSuffix>preview.1</VersionSuffix>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
